<TopicPage slug="subjects/music/intermediate/scales-and-keys">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Scales are <strong>selected subsets of the twelve available notes, organised around a home
    note</strong> — the palette a piece of music paints from. Keys give scales a name and a
    centre: every melody you hum lives in a key, every key change is a felt event, and the
    whole system is a cultural agreement about which frequency ratios sound resolved.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="from twelve equal steps to the systems cultures built on them" widget="equationStep" config={keyCfg} />

  <h2>Why scales shape feeling</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The tonic is gravity:</strong> melodies pull toward the home note; ending elsewhere leaves the ear suspended. Composers delay the tonic to build expectation — resolution is the oldest emotional device in music.</li>
    <li><strong>Interval flavour:</strong> the minor third sounds sombre, the major third bright, the tritone ("diabolus in musica") unstable — each ratio's overtone relationships are heard, not just labelled.</li>
    <li><strong>Blue notes bend the rules:</strong> blues and jazz flatten 3rds and 7ths between the fixed keys — expressive zones Western notation approximates badly. The sound the notation system struggles with is often the most human part.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Benson, Music: A Mathematical Offering; Isacoff, Temperament; Indian raga and Arabic maqam theory sources.</p>
</TopicPage>
